<Base title="Cybernetics in one minute" active="/minute/" description="A sensor gives you a reading. A feedback loop helps you decide what to do next."><article class="post"><header class="post-head"><a class="kicker" href="/minute/">OneWater Minute / 01</a><h1>A reading is<br/><em>only the beginning.</em></h1><p class="post-byline">Systems &amp; Control · 1 min read</p></header><div class="prose"><p>A wet well fills. A sensor notices. A pump starts. The level falls. The next reading changes what happens next.</p><p>That is a feedback loop. It is also a useful way to think about the decisions made far above the pump station.</p><p>You fund a capital program. Which condition is it supposed to improve? What did that condition look like before the work? What happens to the trend afterward?</p><blockquote>A static KPI tells you a number. A cybernetic KPI tells you whether what you are doing is working.</blockquote><p>The missing step is often the last one: letting the result change the next decision. Without it, a dashboard is just a more attractive report.</p><p><strong>One question for the next meeting:</strong> what observation would make us change course?</p><p class="source-note">Adapted from <a href="/writing/vital-signs/">Your Capital Plan Needs a Glucose Monitor</a>.</p></div></article><section class="post-end"><a class="text-link" href="/lexicon/cybernetics/">Explore cybernetics in the lexicon ↗</a></section></Base>
